description:
Create a Claude Desktop configuration example showing how to connect to the notebooklm-mcp server. This is the most common agent host users will use.

CONTENT (docs/examples/claude-desktop-config.json or similar):
{
  'mcpServers': {
    'notebooklm': {
      'command': 'python',
      'args': ['-m', 'notebooklm_mcp'],
      'env': {
        'NOTEBOOKLM_HOME': '/path/to/notebooklm/home',
        'NOTEBOOKLM_MCP_ENABLE_DESTRUCTIVE_TOOLS': '0',
        'NOTEBOOKLM_MCP_LOG_LEVEL': 'INFO'
      }
    }
  }
}

ALSO INCLUDE:
- Pre-requisites (pip install, playwright login)
- Common troubleshooting (auth expired, STDIO corruption)
- Which env vars to set for different use cases
- How to verify the connection works

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA:
- Config is valid JSON and works with Claude Desktop
- Instructions are complete (a user can follow from zero to working)
- Troubleshooting section covers top 3 failure modes

Implement source management MCP tools in tools/sources.py. Sources are the knowledge base of a notebook — URLs, files, text, YouTube, Drive links.

TOOLS (from PLAN-MCP.md §6.2):

1. notebooklm_sources_list
   - Input: { notebook_id: string }
   - Output: { sources: [{ source_id, title, kind, status, created_at? }] }
   - Calls: client.sources.list(notebook_id)

2. notebooklm_sources_add_url
   - Input: { notebook_id: string, url: string, wait?: bool (default true), timeout_ms?: int, poll_interval_ms?: int }
   - Output: { source_id: string, status: string }
   - Calls: client.sources.add_url(notebook_id, url, wait=wait)
   - IMPORTANT: 'wait' parameter with bounded polling avoids LLM-side polling loops (§6.2)

3. notebooklm_sources_add_text
   - Input: { notebook_id: string, title: string, content: string }
   - Output: { source_id: string, status: string }
   - Calls: client.sources.add_text(notebook_id, title, content)

4. notebooklm_sources_add_file
   - Input: { notebook_id: string, filename: string, mime_type: string, data_base64: string, title?: string }
   - Output: { source_id: string, status: string }
   - Calls: client.sources.add_file(...)
   - NOTE: base64 encoding is required for MCP transport (binary not supported in JSON-RPC)

5. notebooklm_sources_remove (DESTRUCTIVE — gated)
   - Input: { notebook_id: string, source_id: string, confirm: bool }
   - Output: { success: true }
   - Gated: NOTEBOOKLM_MCP_ENABLE_DESTRUCTIVE_TOOLS=1 AND confirm=true

6. notebooklm_sources_wait_ready
   - Input: { notebook_id: string, timeout_ms?: int (default 60000), poll_interval_ms?: int (default 1500) }
   - Output: { ready: bool, statuses: [{ source_id, status }] }
   - Polls source statuses until all are ready or timeout
   - Uses bounded polling to prevent runaway waits

7. notebooklm_sources_get_content
   - Input: { notebook_id: string, source_id: string, max_chars?: int }
   - Output: { content: string (truncated to max_chars), truncated: bool }
   - Calls: client.sources.get_fulltext(notebook_id, source_id)
   - Enforces SOURCE_CONTENT_MAX_CHARS cap

DESIGN NOTES:
- wait_ready uses asyncio.sleep polling loop with timeout
- add_url's built-in wait parameter eliminates agent-side polling
- File upload: decode base64 → bytes, pass to client
- All tools use @handle_mcp_errors wrapper

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA:
- All 7 tools registered and functional
- wait_ready properly times out and returns partial statuses
- add_url with wait=true blocks until ready (bounded by timeout)
- File upload handles base64 decoding errors gracefully
- Remove tool properly gated
- Content truncation works and reports truncated=true
